# Input Processing

## Overview

* From Raw Data to Model Input

- @Motivation@: raw data rarely arrives in the shape a model needs, a fixed
  sequence of transformations turns it into clean, well-scaled, informative
  input
  - Improves model performance, generalization, and training stability

- @Techniques@: this lesson covers, in order
  - _Data quality_: remove duplicates and errors, denoise, handle outliers
    and missing values
  - _Scaling and encoding_: put numeric features on comparable scales, turn
    categories into numbers, bin continuous values
  - _Feature space engineering_: construct new features, reduce
    dimensionality
  - _Safe application_: fit every transformation on train only, then
    augment the training set

```graphviz[width=95%]
digraph InputProcessingPipeline {
  bgcolor="transparent";
  pad="0.15";
  splines=spline;
  nodesep=0.28;
  ranksep=0.4;
  rankdir=LR;

  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6,
        fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, margin="0.14,0.10", height=0.42];
  edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.2, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.65,
        fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

  raw   [label="Raw Data", fillcolor="#FFD1A6", color="#D9902B", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
  clean [label="Clean &\nDenoise", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
  fix   [label="Handle Outliers\n& Missing Values", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
  scale [label="Scale &\nEncode", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
  eng   [label="Construct &\nReduce Features", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
  aug   [label="Augment", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
  ready [label="Model-Ready\nData", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];

  raw -> clean -> fix -> scale -> eng -> aug -> ready;
}
```

## Data Quality

* Data Cleaning

- @Goal@: ensure data quality for accurate model training by detecting and correcting
  _errors or inconsistencies_ in the dataset

- @Techniques@:
  - _Remove duplicates_: eliminate identical records
  - _Correct data entry errors_: fix misspellings or misformatted entries
  - _Standardize data_:
    - Convert dates formatted as both `MM/DD/YYYY` and `DD-MM-YYYY` into one
      consistent format
      - E.g., `01/02/2024` is ambiguous between the two formats, so the bug
        is invisible on any single row
    - Normalize strings (e.g., lowercase conversion)
    - Convert types (e.g., strings to integers)
    - Handle unexpected characters or encodings

- @Remark@: removing mislabeled and noisy records lowers both the variance
  of the fitted model and the irreducible error floor

* Noise Removal and Smoothing

- @Goal@: remove irrelevant or corrupt variation from a signal to improve
  clarity and model robustness
  - E.g., clean noisy speech by removing high-frequency noise

- @Techniques@:
  - _Smoothing_: moving average over a window of samples
  - _Filtering_: low-pass filter for audio, median filter for spikes

- @Cons@:
  - A moving average removes real signal along with noise
  - A low-pass filter adds lag between the true and the smoothed value
  - A centered window uses future samples, which is leakage in any
    forecasting setup

* Outliers

- @Definition@: a data point that falls outside $[Q_1 - 1.5 \cdot IQR,
  Q_3 + 1.5 \cdot IQR]$ (the interquartile range rule
  `#cite("tukey1977eda")`{=typst}) or has $|z| > 3$
  - _Causes_: measurement errors, or genuine variability in the data

- @Techniques@:
  - _Detection_: box plots, the IQR rule, or the Z-score
    - The Z-score test assumes approximate normality and is itself
      distorted by the outliers it is trying to find (masking)
  - _Treatment_: removal, capping, or a variance-stabilizing transform
    (e.g., log scale)

- @Remark@: an outlier can be an _error_ or a _rare true event_
  - In fraud detection, anomaly detection, and risk modeling, the outlier
    _is_ the signal, decide which case applies before removing anything

* Missing Data and Imputation

- @Detection@: count of null values or incomplete entries per column

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Definition@: three mechanisms explain why a value is missing
  `#cite("rubin1976missingdata")`{=typst}, and which one holds decides whether
  imputation is safe
  - _MCAR_ (missing completely at random): missingness unrelated to any
    variable, imputation is unbiased
  - _MAR_ (missing at random): missingness depends on observed variables,
    imputation conditioned on those variables is unbiased
  - _MNAR_ (missing not at random): missingness depends on the missing value
    itself
    - E.g., high earners skip the income question, imputation is biased unless
      the mechanism is modeled explicitly
::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

```graphviz[width=85%]
digraph MissingnessMechanisms {
  graph [rankdir=LR, splines=curved, bgcolor="transparent",
         ranksep="0.9 equally", nodesep=0.22, pad=0.25, fontname="Helvetica"];
  node  [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11,
         fontcolor="#26215C", color="#D8D6EE", penwidth=1.1,
         height=0.42, margin="0.16,0.08"];
  edge  [arrowhead=none, penwidth=1.3, color="#B9B6D6"];

  root [label="Why is it missing?", shape=box, style="rounded,filled",
        fillcolor="#26215C", fontcolor="white", fontsize=12, penwidth=0,
        margin="0.22,0.14"];

  mcar [label=<<b>MCAR</b><br/><font point-size="9" color="#1F4E2E">Unrelated to any variable<br/>imputation unbiased</font>>,
        fillcolor="#E5F4EE", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
  mar  [label=<<b>MAR</b><br/><font point-size="9" color="#1F4E79">Depends on observed variables<br/>condition on them, unbiased</font>>,
        fillcolor="#E8F1FB", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
  mnar [label=<<b>MNAR</b><br/><font point-size="9" color="#6B1F1F">Depends on the missing value itself<br/>biased unless modeled</font>>,
        fillcolor="#FBE5E5", color="#D98C8C", fontcolor="#6B1F1F"];

  root -> mcar [color="#8FB79A"];
  root -> mar  [color="#7CA6CE"];
  root -> mnar [color="#D98C8C"];

  { rank=same; mcar; mar; mnar; }
}
```
::::
:::

* Missing Data and Imputation

- @Techniques@:
  - **Deletion**: remove rows or columns with too many missing values
  - **Imputation**: mean/median/mode substitution, K-nearest neighbors (KNN), or a
    regression/model-based estimate
    - E.g., impute a missing temperature reading by interpolating between the day's
      surrounding readings
  - **Missingness indicator**: add a binary `was_missing` column, missingness is
    often predictive on its own

- @Cons@: mean/median/mode substitution
  - Shrinks feature variance
  - Attenuates correlations
  - Bias downstream estimates toward zero

## Feature Scaling and Encoding

* Feature Scaling

- @Goal@: put features on comparable scales for fair distance comparisons
  and faster, more stable optimization
  - _Normalize_ (min-max): rescale to [0, 1]
  - _Standardize_ (z-score): zero mean, unit variance

- @Techniques@:
  - _Min-max normalization_: $x' = \frac{x - x_{\min}}{x_{\max} - x_{\min}}$
  - Z-score standardization: $x' = \frac{x - \mu}{\sigma}$
  - RobustScaler: center and scale by the median and IQR instead of the
    mean and range, unaffected by outliers

- @Comparison@:
  - Min-max is affected by a single extreme value and does not bound unseen test
    values to [0, 1]
  - Z-score is more robust, but is still pulled by heavy outliers
  - RobustScaler is more outlier-safe

* Feature Scaling

- @Comparison@: scale-sensitive vs scale-invariant models

  - _Needs scaling_
    - kNN, SVM, k-means, PCA, and gradient-based models
    - Unscaled feature with a small range gets penalized more heavily by an L1/L2
      penalty, the penalty is not scale-invariant
  - _Invariant to scaling_
    - Tree-based models (decision trees, random forests, gradient boosting) only
      compare feature values via thresholds

* Categorical Encoding

- @Goal@: convert non-numeric categories into numeric representations

- @Techniques@:
  - _Label encoding_: assign an integer to each category
    - E.g., `red`, `green`, `blue` $\to$ `1`, `2`, `3`
    - Asserts both an order and equal spacing that is usually not meaningful
      - E.g., `green - red = blue - green`
  - _One-hot encoding_: create a binary vector per category
    - E.g., `red`, `green`, `blue` $\to$ `[1,0,0]`, `[0,1,0]`, `[0,0,1]`
    - Avoids the ordinal assumption
  - _Ordinal encoding_: label encoding is correct when the order _is_ real
    - E.g., `small < medium < large`
  - _Target/mean encoding_, _learned embeddings_: map each category to a statistic of
    the target, or to a learned dense vector

- @Cons@:
  - One-hot increases dimensionality sharply for high-cardinality columns
  - A category unseen at inference time has no learned encoding, map it to an
    explicit `unknown` bucket

* Discretization

- @Goal@: convert a continuous quantity to categorical


- @Techniques@:
  - _Equal-width binning_: split the range into bins of equal width
  - _Quantile binning_: split so each bin holds an equal count of points
  - _Supervised binning_: choose cut points with a decision tree on the
    target
  - _K-means binning_: cluster values into bins along one dimension

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Example@: discretizing age
  - `Child`: [0, 13), `Teen`: [13, 20), `Adult`: [20, 65), `Senior`: [65,
    $\infty$)
  - Age 32 $\to$ `Adult`
- @Cons@:
  - Discards all within-bin variation, e.g., ages 21 and 64 both become `Adult`
  - Cut points are arbitrary
  - An equal-width bin can end up empty
  - A real threshold effect can fall inside a bin instead of at its edge
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

```graphviz[width=90%]
digraph AgeBinning {
  bgcolor="transparent";
