  senior [label="Senior\n[65, inf)", fillcolor="#E8F1FB", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

  child -> teen -> adult -> senior [style=invis];
  { rank=same; child; teen; adult; senior; }

  age32 [label="Age = 32", shape=ellipse, fillcolor="#FFD1A6", color="#D9902B", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
  age32 -> adult [label="mapped to", color="#D9902B", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
}
```
::::
:::

## Feature Space Engineering

* Feature Construction

- @Goal@:
  - Derive more informative features from raw inputs
  - Encod domain knowledge the raw columns don't expose directly

- @Techniques@:
  - Combine variables (e.g., $area = height \times width$)
  - Extract parts (e.g., year from a date)
  - Derive logical features
    - E.g., transform `2023-04-15` $\to$ (`Saturday`, `is_weekend = True`)

- @Remark@: hand-built features are the part representation learning in deep learning
  replaces

- @Cons@: a feature built from the target, or from rows that occur after the
  prediction point in time, leaks _information_

* Dimensionality Reduction

- @Goal@: reduce the number of features while preserving key information, e.g.,
  - Reduce 1024x640 image pixels to 10 principal components
  - Collapse 3 color channels into 1 luminance (grayscale) channel

- @Comparison@: feature selection vs feature extraction
  - _Selection_: keep a subset of the original, interpretable columns
  - _Extraction_ (e.g., PCA): build new combinations of columns that are no
    longer directly interpretable

- @Techniques@:
  - _PCA_ `#cite("pearson1901pca")`{=typst}: unsupervised, linear combinations
    that maximize variance
  - _LDA_: supervised, projects data to maximize class separability
  - _t-SNE_ `#cite("vandermaaten2008tsne")`{=typst}, _UMAP_
    `#cite("mcinnes2018umap")`{=typst}: non-linear methods used mainly for
    visualization
  - Some techniques are scale dependent

- @Applications@
  - Reduce overfitting
  - Reduce data redundancy
  - Improve model speed
  - Allow visualization

## Applying Transformations Safely

* Fit on Train, Apply to Validation and Test

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Problem@: a model, a scaler, imputer, encoder, and PCA are all _fitted_ objects,
  each learns statistics from data
  - E.g., mean, min/max, category list, principal axes
  - Fitting them on the full dataset (before splitting) leaks information into
    training `#cite("kaufman2012leakage")`{=typst}
::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

```graphviz
digraph FitTransformLeakage {
  bgcolor="transparent";
  pad="0.15";
  splines=spline;
  nodesep=0.3;
  ranksep=0.4;
  rankdir=LR;

  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.5,
        fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, margin="0.14,0.10", height=0.42];
  edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.2, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.6,
        fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

  subgraph cluster_wrong {
    label     = "Wrong: fit before split";
    labelloc  = "t";
    fontname  = "Helvetica-Bold";
    fontsize  = 11;
    fontcolor = "#6B1F1F";
    style     = "rounded,filled";
    fillcolor = "#FBE5E5";
    color     = "#D98C8C";
    margin    = 14;

    all_data [label="All Data", fillcolor="#FFD1A6", color="#D9902B", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    fit_all  [label="Fit scaler/imputer\non ALL rows", fillcolor="#FBC6C6", color="#D64545", fontcolor="#6B1F1F"];
    split_w  [label="Split", fillcolor="#FBC6C6", color="#D64545", fontcolor="#6B1F1F"];
    train_w  [label="Train", fillcolor="#F6C6C6", color="#D98C8C", fontcolor="#6B2A2A"];
    test_w   [label="Test\n(already leaked into)", fillcolor="#F6C6C6", color="#D98C8C", fontcolor="#6B2A2A"];

    all_data -> fit_all -> split_w;
    split_w -> train_w;
    split_w -> test_w;
  }
}
```
::::
:::

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Solution@: fit every transformer on the training fold only, then apply the
  already-fitted transformer to validation and test
  - Repeat this inside every cross-validation fold, not once for the whole
    dataset
  - E.g., `sklearn.pipeline.Pipeline` chains the fitted steps
::::
:::: {.column width=50%}
```graphviz
digraph FitTransformLeakage {
  bgcolor="transparent";
  pad="0.15";
  splines=spline;
  nodesep=0.3;
  ranksep=0.4;
  rankdir=LR;

  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.5,
        fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, margin="0.14,0.10", height=0.42];
  edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.2, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.6,
        fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

  subgraph cluster_correct {
    label     = "Correct: split before fit";
    labelloc  = "t";
    fontname  = "Helvetica-Bold";
    fontsize  = 11;
    fontcolor = "#1F4E2E";
    style     = "rounded,filled";
    fillcolor = "#E5F4EE";
    color     = "#8FB79A";
    margin    = 14;

    all_data2 [label="All Data", fillcolor="#FFD1A6", color="#D9902B", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    split_c   [label="Split", fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    train_c   [label="Train", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
    fit_c     [label="Fit scaler/imputer\non TRAIN only", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];
    test_c    [label="Test", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    apply_c   [label="Apply fitted\ntransform", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    all_data2 -> split_c;
    split_c -> train_c -> fit_c;
    split_c -> test_c -> apply_c;
    fit_c -> apply_c [style=dashed, label="reuse", color="#6FA890"];
  }
}
```

::::
:::

* Data Augmentation

- @Goal@: increase the effective size and diversity of a dataset by
  applying label-preserving transformations to existing examples

- @Techniques@:
  - _Vision_: random crop, flip, rotation, color jitter, cutout
  - _Text_: synonym replacement, back-translation, random word dropout
  - _Audio_: pitch shift, time stretch, added background noise
  - _Tabular_: SMOTE-style synthetic minority oversampling
    `#cite("chawla2002smote")`{=typst}

- @Remark@: augmentation acts as a "regularizer"
  - It exposes the model to more variation than the raw dataset contains, without
    collecting new labels

- @Cons@:
  - An augmentation that changes the true label trains the model on wrong data
    - E.g., flipping an image of a `6` into a `9`
  - Make sure the synthetic data has the same distribution of the true data

